    "acceptance-spiked": "Spiked directly against the real binary, never a core function below its entry point — driven via `node -e` calling `createService()` + `FixedClock` + `router.dispatch(\"POST\", \"/samples\", ...)` + `service.tick()`, the same entry points a real caller uses. Two behaviors observed: (a) posting 50,000 distinct series names, ticking every 500 posts so the ingest queue never overflows first, leaves `store.series().length === 50000` today (and `size() === 50000`) — confirms no cap exists pre-fix, and gives the exact repro Increment 2's acceptance test asserts against post-fix (same drive, a capped count expected instead of 50000). (b) advancing a `FixedClock` past `retentionMs` and calling `tick()` manually correctly drops all samples (`store.size()` 10000 → 0), confirming `prune()`'s arithmetic itself is correct and the gap is purely about *invocation frequency*, not the pruning logic.",
    "census": "Two greps, full hit-counts recorded. (1) `grep -rniE \"cap|evict|LRU|interval|schedule|setInterval|cardinality\" src test` → 8 lines / 1 real cluster (`IngestQueue.capacity`, unrelated to series count) — confirms no partial cap/scheduling machinery exists anywhere to account for. (2) `grep -rn \"\\.put(\" src test` → 3 call sites total: test/rollup.test.ts:59-60 (2 calls) and src/index.ts:38 (the only production call site, inside `tick()`'s drain loop) — both ignore `put()`'s return value today, so changing `put()` from returning `void` to `boolean` is call-site-safe everywhere the grep found it, and nothing else calls it (no re-export or alias exists — `grep -rn \"MemoryStore\" src` shows a single class definition and import sites only). The new cap property is enforced at exactly one point: `grep -n \"buckets.set\" src/store.ts` → 1 hit (store.ts:26, inside `put()`), the sole place a new key is ever admitted — so the cap is enforced at a constructor-adjacent single decision point, not a list of call sites that could rot.",
    "cheap-vs-robust": "Two forks, each argued by an independent robust-case advocate who proposed neither cheap option. Fork A — series-cap admission: reject-hard a brand-new series once a `maxSeries` limit is hit, versus LRU-evicting an existing idle series to make room. Settled on reject-hard: LRU is the harder-to-reverse one-way door here, because it ships a system where a caller cannot distinguish \"series went idle\" from \"series got silently evicted\" — correcting that later (adding a legible signal) is a breaking behavior change, whereas reject-hard is already legible and safe to loosen into a smarter policy later if product needs it. This service's committed trajectory is to eventually sit behind a real transport (`router.ts` is documented \"transport-agnostic on purpose\"), so the failure mode under real client load matters now, not hypothetically. Fork B — aging m

